{-
   `'` is a token of its own - `doc/spec/lexical.md`'s origin marker.

   It was a *symbol* until now, and a reserved operator is only recognized when it is a symbol run of
   its own length - so `%'a`, `'%a` and `&'a` were each one unknown operator, and each reported
   "expected a type" with nothing said about why. Every spelling below is one that did not lex.

   What the marker means is one thing in all of them, and it is Analysis-Borrows-Revised.md §2.1's
   table: the tick **publishes an origin**, and the origin is the signature's anonymous one unless a
   label names another. `&` is the separate axis of whether the value is mutable, so the two compose
   in every combination and each is read once.
-}

data Cell {v: Int}

-- `&T` and `&'T` are two contracts and not one type written twice, which is what the printed
-- signatures below assert: an unticked `&` parameter is a borrow bounded by the call, and a ticked
-- one publishes slot 0 for a result or a field to name. That is the same difference `T` and `'T`
-- have at a shared parameter, said for the exclusive axis - `&` decides the access and `'` the
-- origin, and neither implies the other.
fn viaShorthand(c: &Cell) -> Int:
    c.v = c.v + 1
    return c.v

fn viaMarker(c: &'Cell) -> Int:
    c.v = c.v + 1
    return c.v

-- The named form of the same slot. The capability is written outside the origin, which is a
-- spelling choice and now only that: it had to be, while `&'` was one unknown operator.
fn viaLabel(c: &owner'Cell) -> Int:
    c.v = c.v + 1
    return c.v

-- And the bracketed spellings, which are the same types with grouping around them rather than a
-- second constructor: `&('Cell)` is `&'Cell`, tick and all. It built a borrow of a borrow and was
-- accepted in silence by both stages until one rule read both spellings - `Parser::applySigil`,
-- which is also where the tick survives the fold.
fn viaBrackets(c: &('Cell)) -> Int:
    c.v = c.v + 1
    return c.v

fn viaBracketedLabel(c: &(owner'Cell)) -> Int:
    c.v = c.v + 1
    return c.v

fn main() -> Int:
    let &a = Cell {v: 1}
    let &b = Cell {v: 10}
    let &c = Cell {v: 100}
    let &d = Cell {v: 1000}
    let &e = Cell {v: 10000}
    return viaShorthand(a) + viaMarker(b) + viaLabel(c) + viaBrackets(d) + viaBracketedLabel(e)
